A reviewer claims this security issue:
  title: SQL Injection via string concatenation
  lines: 11-11 (new file)
  rationale: The `id` parameter is concatenated directly into a raw SQL string without any sanitization or parameterization. An attacker who controls `id` can break out of the string literal — e.g. `' OR '1'='1` — to read arbitrary rows, or use `'; DROP TABLE users; --` for destructive operations. Because `findUser` is a public method accepting a caller-supplied string, this is trivially reachable.

